From 4a1d4a2f4b4d9a72e4d6b9dc816a34bd613ea1f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bob Van Valzah <Bob@vanvalzah.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 16:35:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Add external oscillator control mode (for host-driven
 discipline)

Adds an opt-in mode that lets an external host discipline the oscillator
directly instead of the built-in GNSSDO loop, over the existing ESP32
console (USB or the mosaic-T TCP backdoor). Written for the PePPAR-Fix
GNSS-disciplined-clock project but kept generic.

New line-based console commands (any line beginning with '$'; every other
keystroke still opens the interactive menu):

  $E,1 / $E,0   enable/disable external control
  $W,<word>     write the oscillator frequency control word (absolute);
                implicitly enables external control and re-arms the watchdog
  $T,<seconds>  set the fail-safe watchdog timeout
  $R            report status (ext flag, control word, bias, state, watchdog)

While external control is active the firmware holds a new
STATE_GNSS_EXTERNAL_CONTROL, never calls updateTCXO() (a hard interlock in
updateTCXO() backs this up), and keeps PPS and clock-bias telemetry running.

Fail-safe: a watchdog (default 15 s, settable via $T or Operation menu item
52, persisted) requires a discipline command at least that often. If the
host goes quiet the firmware resets its servo integrator and resumes
internal discipline from the oscillator's current control word. The
external-control flag is never persisted, so a power cycle also returns the
unit to internal discipline.

To make the clean hand-back possible, the STP3593LF PI(D) integrator state
was moved from function statics into the driver object, so rebuilding the
driver (resetTcxoIntegrator()) reseeds it from the current control word for
every oscillator type.
